A||N||7||TheFiero64||HELP! Ball Joint boot||||5||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||11-27-2000||05:23 PM||||I need to know the size of the ball joint boots and the Tie rod boot on an 88. Please I just split mine with a pickle fork. The joints are still good. Here is the link to Jegs. They have a few different sizes. The are all made out of red poly. Thanks in advance.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com/cgi-bin/ncommerce3/ProductDisplay?prrfnbr=2467&prmenbr=76&path=76%202187%202188" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jegs.com/cgi-bin/ncommerce3/ProductDisplay?prrfnbr=2467&prmenbr=76&path=76%202187%202188</A> <P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||11-27-2000||07:16 PM||||I don't know the exact size but you can get the Tie Rod end boots from Suspension Restoration Parts Co.  Thier web site is <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> <P>If you call them they might be able to provide the ball joint boots as well.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-27-2000||08:15 PM||||Can't help on the dimensions. You can get the dimension with a piece of stiff wire. bend it into a C shape and adjust it untill it just drags the sides of the joint then measure the gap.<P>I don't use a fork on anything but junk joints. <P>Next time find one of the dimented C clamp things that presses the rod ends out by the stem. Those won't wreck the boots. It looks like a small beefy C clamp with the bottom end forked. <P>On the front ball joints you want the bolt thing described in the various books or the one Rodney has. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||11-27-2000||09:23 PM||||The same happened to me after seperating the ball joint.  A mechanic friend of mine suggested super glue to repair the rubber.  I laughed but figured "What the heck". After removing the rubber, I cleaned it spotless with lacquer thinner, paying particular attention to the tears. Glued the cracks and repaced the rubber.  Months later I repaced the upper A-arm bushings a closely inspected the rubbers and they were still perfect. Pays to listen to a veteran mechanic. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-27-2000||09:37 PM||||Super glue is weird. It works really great or doesn't work worth a turd. No middle ground with that stuff.<P>I didn't even think of using it for this. Probably should have, we used to use it to make custom belts for fixing AV equipment.||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||11-27-2000||09:38 PM||||Super glue work great on two things, rubber and skin.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TheFiero64||11-28-2000||10:53 AM||||Thanks everyone. I will glue it today and order the boots. I know the tie rod one. I will have to find ball joint ones||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero5||11-28-2000||11:25 AM||||Ya gotta love it! Glue is the answer for a technical car part question.<BR>Anybody ever used "Automotive Goop". That stuff is awesome!!!||||reg||1
